"""Narrative of report 19 (Milestone 11, Phases 1 and 2 on the Panda; ADR 0011). The result sections are filled from
runs/m11/metrics_p*.json (the profile ablation), the sweep files, metrics_tasks_p0.json (the pick with a grasp),
floor_hold.json (the error floor), the iface_* logs (the learned interface) and probe_panda.json (the probes).
The standard group sections of d_report show profile P1 (the next robot's interface)."""
from experiments.d7_extras import LABELS
from experiments.m11_extras import floor_table, interface_table, load_profiles, nad_table, pick_table, probe_table, profile_table, rate_table, retune_table, stiffness_table, sweep_figure, torque_table, velfilter_table

_profiles = load_profiles()
ORDER = ["best_classical", "pd_shared", "pd_shared_cap", "pd_feedforward", "pd_shared_cap_feedforward", "oracle_pd"]
METHODS = [(k, LABELS.get(k, k)) for k in ORDER]

ABLATION_SECTION = ("<h2>The interface ablation</h2>\n<p>The same classical controllers through the four profiles, the model-based interface mapping every frame (decision 3 of ADR 0011). The first table follows the best classical controller of each family (chosen per profile), the second the feedforward PD alone, the third the privileged oracle, which here is the ceiling under the interface rather than a baseline.</p>\n"
                    + profile_table(_profiles) + profile_table(_profiles, "pd_feedforward") + profile_table(_profiles, "oracle_pd")) if _profiles else ""
TORQUE_SECTION = ("<h2>The torque-tracking family</h2>\n" + torque_table(_profiles)) if _profiles and any("torque_tracking" in g for g in _profiles.values()) else ""
_sweep = sweep_figure() if _profiles else ""
SWEEP_SECTION = ("<h2>Host period and bus delay</h2>\n" + _sweep) if _sweep else ""
STIFFNESS_SECTION = ("<h2>Effective stiffness</h2>\n" + stiffness_table(_profiles)) if _profiles and "p0" in _profiles else ""
_retune, _velfilter, _rate = retune_table(), velfilter_table(), rate_table()
RETUNE_SECTION = ("<h2>The PD re-tuned through each profile</h2>\n<p>The classical answer to the interface: the shared PD gains re-tuned by CMA-ES through the profile's servo loop (the same budget and objective as the Milestone 9 tune, the feedforward form), with the servo's nominal spring set to the re-tuned gains.</p>\n" + _retune) if _retune else ""
VELFILTER_SECTION = ("<h2>The velocity-feedback assumption</h2>\n<p>Every torque-rate figure through the profiles rests on the servo damping on the raw quantised velocity it reports. A profile parameter puts a first-order filter on that estimate (the servo damps on it, the host sees it); the sweep says how much of the smoothness cost is the assumption's; the second table removes the interface's sources of roughness one at a time.</p>\n" + _velfilter + _rate) if _velfilter else ""
_floor, _iface, _pick, _probe, _nad = floor_table(), interface_table(), pick_table(), probe_table(), nad_table()
NAD_SECTION = ("<h2>Phase 0: what public servo data identifies</h2>\n" + _nad) if _nad else ""
FLOOR_SECTION = ("<h2>The error floor of the model-based interface</h2>\n" + _floor) if _floor else ""
IFACE_SECTION = ("<h2>The learned interface</h2>\n" + _iface) if _iface else ""
_videos = (__import__("pathlib").Path("research/reports/19-m11-interfaces/data/videos.html"))
PICK_SECTION = ("<h2>Pick-and-place with a grasp</h2>\n" + _pick + (_videos.read_text() if _videos.exists() else "")) if _pick else ""
PROBE_SECTION = ("<h2>What the representations encode</h2>\n" + _probe) if _probe else ""

NARRATIVE = {
    "question": """<p>The arms the project wants to reach next expose no torque command. The i2rt YAM takes an impedance frame on CAN at 250 Hz (position, velocity, stiffness, damping and a feedforward torque, the MIT mode of its Damiao motors); Dynamixel arms take a goal position with RAM-writable gains and a current cap over a serial bus at 100 to 500 Hz; Feetech arms take a goal position with gains fixed in EEPROM at 50 to 200 Hz. The question of this phase is what each interface costs a controller that was designed for a 1 kHz torque command, measured on the plant where the record stands (the Panda with the Robotiq 2F-85, protocol v3) so that the interface is the only change, and whether a learned layer on the interface's channels can recover any of it (hypotheses P1 and P2 of ADR 0011). Two protocol additions come with it: a torque-tracking family that measures the interface alone, and a pick-and-place family that includes the pick.</p>""",
    "setup": """<p><strong>Profiles.</strong> P0 is the torque interface of reports 01 to 18. P1 models MIT mode on CAN: a frame every 4 ms with a 1 ms bus delay, 16-bit position and 12-bit velocity feedback, the gains and a feedforward torque in every frame (Kp up to 6.25 times the servo's nominal stiffness, Kd up to the nominal damping, 12 bits each). P2 models a Dynamixel in position mode: a goal position every 10 ms with a 2 ms delay, 4096 counts per revolution, velocity feedback in steps of 24 mrad/s, P and D gains written per frame, a current cap. P3 models a Feetech servo: a goal position every 20 ms with a 3 ms delay, the same position resolution, velocity in steps of 77 mrad/s, fixed gains, a torque limit. On the Panda the servo's own loop is the tuned PD law at 1 kHz on the quantised measurement with the Panda's torque limits as the cap; the DC-motor layer of the real arms is not part of this ablation.</p>
<p><strong>Model-based interface.</strong> At each host instant the upper controller is called with the quantised feedback and returns its impedance frame; the interface advances the reference over the bus delay, passes the frame through on P1 (the part of the command beyond the gain channels' range goes into the feedforward torque) and folds it into the goal position on P2 and P3 so that the servo's torque equals the command at the frame's predicted arrival. This is how the shipped drivers of these arms are used, so it is the classical baseline through every profile.</p>
<p><strong>Controllers and benchmark.</strong> The Milestone 10 classical set (the tracking-tuned and the cap-tuned PD, their inverse-dynamics feedforward variants) and the privileged oracle around the tracking-tuned PD, on the in-distribution families, the task families and the compliant 500 N/m surfaces of protocol v3 (16 cases per family), plus the torque-tracking family of protocol v4: a hold reference with a torque program on the upper command (hold, steps of 10 to 30% of the stall torque on one joint, a chirp from 0.5 to 125 Hz at 10%, a pure-torque chirp at 2%), the realised joint torque compared with the command evaluated at the true state at every physics step. The sweep varies the host period (2 to 40 ms at a 2 ms delay) and the bus delay (0 to 10 ms at a 10 ms period) of the P2 structure under the feedforward PD.</p>
<p><strong>Learned interface (Phase 2a).</strong> One network per profile (four layers of 256, a history of ten host frames of measured state, realised torque and the goal sent) emitting bounded corrections on the profile's channels (a goal offset within 50 mrad, gain multipliers within 0.5 to 2 where the gains are writable, a feedforward residual within 20% of the limits where the channel exists), applied on top of the model-based interface and exactly equal to it at the start (zero head). Trained by backpropagation through the plant (finite-difference gradients, median clipping) on the loss between the realised torque and the upper command at the true state plus a torque-rate penalty, on hold windows with torque programs and free-space and contact windows under the feedforward PD, 200 iterations of 16 windows of 1 s; selected on the validation cases (eight torque-tracking cases and the hold, free and blocked families under the feedforward PD).</p>
<p><strong>Pick-and-place with a grasp.</strong> The block rests on the table; the gripper, baked at a 46 mm opening around the 34 mm block, approaches from 12 cm above, descends to the block's centre, dwells, and the weld activates 0.2 s into the dwell only if the block's centre is within 6 mm laterally and 9 mm along the tool axis of the tool point, at the block's current pose; then lift, carry, place, lift. Grasp success, the block's displacement before the grasp, the tool-block force during the descent and the place metrics of v3 are reported; task success requires the grasp and a placement within 2 cm and 15 degrees.</p>
<p><strong>Probing study.</strong> Linear probes from the observation and from every trunk layer of the Milestone 10 two-teacher student, fitted on even validation episodes and tested on odd ones, to the privileged labels the simulator knows: contact, table against wall, the surface normal in the tool frame, the surface stiffness class, the payload mass and the external force magnitude.</p>""",
    "extra_sections": [s for s in (NAD_SECTION, ABLATION_SECTION, TORQUE_SECTION, SWEEP_SECTION, STIFFNESS_SECTION, RETUNE_SECTION, VELFILTER_SECTION, FLOOR_SECTION, IFACE_SECTION, PICK_SECTION, PROBE_SECTION) if s],
    "summary_intro": "<p>The standard sections below show profile P1 (the YAM's interface); the ablation across profiles is in the tables above.</p>",
    "findings": """<ol>
<li><strong>Phase 0: the servo classes are identifiable from public telemetry to the extent the interface model needs.</strong> On the Dynamixel XM430 the motor current follows the DC-motor law with a winding resistance of 4.8 to 5.4 ohm and a back-EMF constant of 2.4 V s/rad at the joint on four of five joints (R² 0.93 to 0.98), which imply a stall current of 2.2 to 2.5 A and a no-load speed of 47 to 48 rpm against the datasheet's 2.3 A and 46 rpm; the firmware's position loop is not separable at 59 Hz because its integral and derivative terms act on an internal velocity estimate. On the Feetech STS3215 the load register is the P term on the position error (2600 to 3900 per mille of duty per radian, R² 0.97 to 0.999 on the arm joints) with no visible velocity term. Both report velocity in coarse steps (24 and 77 mrad/s), which is what the profiles carry.</li>
<li><strong>The interface ablation: tracking and contact survive every profile, smoothness does not.</strong> Through the model-based interface the best classical controller keeps its tracking on every family (hold 8.1 to 8.2 mrad across the four profiles, free motion 4.9, 4.9, 5.0 and 6.4 mrad, policy-like targets 43 to 45 mrad, steps 36 to 42 mrad) and its contact behaviour (press-and-slide 9.1 to 9.3 N above the cap at every profile, blocked 23 to 24 N, the realised contact stiffness 985 to 1016 N/m against 984 at P0, so the realised fraction of the commanded impedance is 1.00 to 1.03). What changes is the torque rate: 115 Nm/s in free motion at P0, 587 at P1, 1074 at P2, 1710 at P3, and alike on every family. The servo acts on its own quantised velocity feedback (15, 24 and 77 mrad/s steps times a damping of up to 50 Nm s/rad), so the chatter is the interface's, not the controller's. The cap composites therefore grow with the profile where the tracking term is small: hold 1.74, 1.82, 2.13, 2.30; free motion 0.36, 0.83, 1.32, 2.03; policy-like 2.34 to 2.39; steps 3.30 to 3.61. Pre-registered criterion (ii), P1 within 10% of P0 on the free-space composites, is met on hold, policy-like targets and steps (+5%, +0% and +1%) and missed on free motion (+130%), through the torque-rate term alone; P2 and P3 miss free motion by the same mechanism and stay within their 25% and 50% bounds on the other three. No controller diverges on any profile. The privileged oracle keeps its ceiling through P1 (blocked 7.41 against 7.23, 12.7 N above the cap on both) and, through the goal-position profiles, holds blocked targets with less excess force than on the torque interface (2.9 and 3.3 N above the cap at P2 and P3) at a lower realised stiffness (411 against 726 N/m) and a deeper yield (28 against 24 mrad): folded into a goal with the servo's spring and its quantised damping between frames, the impedance law comes out softer than commanded. The ceiling is not monotone in the interface, and the oracle's first rows through the profiles, taken while its frame path still advanced its reference once per frame, were discarded (ADR 0011 amendment).</li>
<li><strong>The torque-tracking family separates the profiles the way the bus does.</strong> The realised torque follows the feedforward PD's command within 0.4 to 1.3% of the stall torque at P1 (delay 1 to 2 ms, chirp bandwidth above 100 Hz), within 2.0 to 2.5% at P2 (delay 2 to 5 ms, bandwidth 30 to 49 Hz) and within 2.1 to 2.8% at P3 (delay 3 to 10 ms, bandwidth 10 to 29 Hz); the pure-torque chirp, a command without a spring to hold between frames, is realised at P2 and P3 only through the servo's own stiffness around a goal offset and keeps a bandwidth of 29 to 49 Hz.</li>
<li><strong>Period and delay: the hold matters beyond 20 ms, the delay hardly at all.</strong> Over the host period of the P2 structure the free-motion composite goes 0.93, 1.04, 1.32, 1.76 and 4.12 at 2, 4, 10, 20 and 40 ms (4.9 mrad up to 10 ms, 6.7 at 20, 44 at 40, where the policy-like targets also break at 68 mrad), hold stays at 2.13 and blocked at 14.2 up to 20 ms; over the bus delay of 0 to 10 ms at a 10 ms period every composite moves by less than 4% (free motion 4.9 to 5.3 mrad). The torque-tracking error grows from 2.0 to 2.5% across both sweeps, its delay follows the bus delay.</li>
<li><strong>The error floor of the model-based interface is the servo's, and the learned interface finds nothing below it.</strong> On hold windows with torque programs the model-based interface realises the command within 1.6, 3.2 and 4.0% of the stall torque at P1, P2 and P3; removing the bus delay or the hold one at a time leaves 0.8 to 1.4% at P1, removing both leaves 0.5% (2.0 and 2.2% at P2 and P3), and only exact feedback as well brings it to zero. On free-space windows the hold is the larger term (P1 5.5% as is, 3.2% without the delay, 4.6% at a 1 ms period, 0.5% with both removed). A scan of constant corrections on the interface's channels (goal offsets of 2 to 4 mrad, damping multipliers of 0.7 to 1.5) raises the loss in every direction from the model-based point. Trained by backpropagation through the plant, the learned interface's torque-tracking error on the validation cases is worse than the model-based warm start's on every profile (P1 0.7% to 1.3%, P2 2.0% to 3.4%, P3 2.0% to 2.2%); its validation mean improves by at most 1% through a lower torque rate and the blocked family. Pre-registered criterion (i), halving the error at no more than 1.5 times the rate, is not met on the Panda ablation: with the reference advanced to the middle of the hold the model-based interface is at the floor that the servo's own loop sets between host frames.</li>
<li><strong>The pick with a grasp discriminates controllers by what they do before the grasp.</strong> On the torque interface the feedforward PD grasps 7 of 16 blocks (the tracking-tuned PD 6, the cap-tuned 5, the oracle 6) and the two-teacher student 10.7 of 16 on average over three seeds. The PD misses every case with the 2 kg tool payload: it sags 10 to 19 mm below and 10 mm beside the commanded grasp point, outside the 6 mm by 9 mm tolerance, with the block undisturbed (0.7 mm) and untouched. The student takes every 1 and 2 kg case and misses four of five cases without payload: it arrives with 4 to 6 mm of lateral error, its pads brush the block during the descent (6 to 9 N on the block, 1 to 2 mm of displacement), and the contact law that wins the blocked family yields, so it stops 18 mm above the grasp height. Through the profiles the classical grasp rate is unchanged (7 of 16 at every profile) and push success falls at P3 (5 to 2 of 16 for the feedforward PD).</li>
<li><strong>The two-layer student's representations encode contact and little else.</strong> Linear probes from the trunk layers of the two-teacher student decode contact at 0.77 to 0.80 balanced accuracy, the observation itself at 0.77 to 0.79; the surface normal in the tool frame is decodable from the observation (R² 0.34 to 0.39) and not from the layers (negative R²); the stiffness classes, the payload and the external force are at chance from both. With a 2 by 128 trunk the student keeps the contact cue it was given and builds no linear representation of geometry, stiffness or load; the deeper host-rate students of Phase 2b are probed next.</li>
<li><strong>The PD re-tuned through each profile buys compliance and smoothness with accuracy.</strong> CMA-ES through the profile's servo loop on the cap composite (the Milestone 9 budget) lowers the stiffness to 0.52, 0.48 and 0.47 of the torque-interface gains through P1, P2 and P3 (the tuning objective 3.33, 3.43 and 3.51 against 5.07, 5.16 and 5.53 for the torque-interface gains through the same profiles), halves the blocked excess force (39.6 to 20.1, 20.0 and 17.6 N above the cap; contact stiffness 1424 to 930, 923 and 873 N/m) and cuts the free-motion torque rate by 42 to 59% (587 to 338, 1074 to 550 and 1710 to 706 Nm/s), at the price of the free-motion error (4.9 to 10.4, 5.0 to 10.5 and 6.4 to 12.3 mrad) and the hold (1.82 to 3.20, 2.13 to 3.44 and 2.30 to 3.97). On the composites it wins blocked targets (14.2 to 9.7, 9.7 and 9.0) and policy-like targets (2.35 to 2.14, 2.39 to 2.04 and 2.38 to 1.94), ties press-and-slide, loses hold and steps (3.32 to 3.67, 3.45 to 3.69 and 3.61 to 3.93), and on free motion loses through P1 (0.83 to 0.86) and wins through P2 and P3 (1.32 to 1.08, 2.03 to 1.32), where the torque-rate term dominates the composite. It joins the classical set of the criterion tables (report 21) as the best classical controller of the blocked and policy-like families through every profile. Its torque rate stays three to six times the torque interface's 115 Nm/s: softening does not recover the torque interface's smoothness.</li>
<li><strong>The smoothness cost is the interface's, not the raw-velocity assumption's.</strong> With a first-order filter on the velocity feedback (2, 5 and 10 ms; the servo damps on, and the host sees, the filtered estimate) the feedforward PD's free-motion torque rate through P1 goes 587 to 540, 533 and 531 Nm/s, the oracle's 599 to 545, 537 and 535 and the host-rate student's 715 to 661, 663 and 730; tracking is unchanged (4.9 mrad), the blocked excess unchanged (39.6 and 23.5 N), and the torque-tracking error grows with the filter's lag (0.76 to 0.78, 0.88 and 1.12% of the stall torque). A tenth of the torque rate is the velocity estimate. The rest is the interface: through the P2 structure the free-motion torque rate grows with the host period (685, 792, 1074, 1429 and 1914 Nm/s at 2, 4, 10, 20 and 40 ms, against 115 at the torque interface) and not with the bus delay (1074, 1076 and 1077 at 0, 5 and 10 ms), while on the hold family it is 490 at every period against 111 at the torque interface: the held setpoint moves by the reference velocity times the period at every frame, a staircase the servo's spring turns into torque steps, on top of a floor that does not depend on the period. Removing the sources one at a time through P1 (the second table) orders them: a frame every millisecond with no bus delay takes the free-motion torque rate from 587 to 263 Nm/s, exact position and velocity feedback alone to 550, both together to 186 against 115 at the torque interface, and the command channels' 12-bit quantisation accounts for the rest only once the other two are gone (removing it alone changes nothing); on the hold family, where the reference is static, the feedback quantisation is the whole gap (185 to 111). Criterion (ii)'s free-motion misses through every profile, and the host-rate students' misses on the fast families through their torque rate, are properties of the position interface as modelled; only a firmware that interpolates the goal between frames would change them.</li>
</ol>""",
    "caveats": """<ul>
<li>The ablation changes the interface only: the servo's position loop is abstracted as the tuned PD in torque units with the Panda's limits as the cap; the DC-motor layer of the real arms (back-EMF, winding resistance, gearbox friction, dead zone) is not modelled here and enters with the robots of Phase 3.</li>
<li>The host periods and bus delays are the shipped drivers' rates and packet-size estimates, not measurements; the sweep covers the plausible range.</li>
<li>The servo's velocity feedback is modelled as the velocity its own loop uses, at the resolution the bus reports (the Dynamixel's reported velocity is its internal estimate); the filter sweep (finding 9) bounds what a firmware that filters its estimate would change, about a tenth of the torque rate. A firmware that interpolates the goal between frames (a velocity profile) would remove the setpoint staircase and is not modelled.</li>
<li>Sixteen cases per family, one seed per learned interface; the torque-tracking family has four cases per program kind.</li>
<li>The probes are linear and fitted across the workspace: the contact force and the surface normal are linear in the joint torques and the tool frame only at a fixed configuration, so a negative R² says the quantity is not linearly readable from the features across configurations, not that the network carries no information about it.</li>
</ul>""",
    "next": """<ul>
<li>The host-rate students of Phase 2b (four layers of 256, decisions at every host frame) are distilled through the three profiles from the oracle and the ideal-robot labels and probed layer by layer (report 21).</li>
<li>The torque rate through a profile is the interface's own (findings 8 and 9); the one modelling choice that could lower it on hardware is a firmware that interpolates the goal between frames, which belongs with the measurements when hardware is allowed.</li>
<li>The YAM (report 20): the same protocol on the arm whose interface is P1, with the gains re-tuned through it, a host-rate student, and the DC-motor layer under the servo law; the Koch arm on the Dynamixel profile is deferred (the advisor's decision of 2026-11-17).</li>
<li>The pick family's zero-payload losses of the student are a contact-law question: a grasp approach should not yield to a 6 N brush against its object. A task-conditioned mode or a smaller press floor during approaches is the natural fix and belongs to the sustained-contact round proposed in the context document.</li>
</ul>""",
}
